#{[17592186549973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549973 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90974" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549973 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549973 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-4x+4?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549973 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7dfd6f87-7029-50ed-aece-aeae8cb98e8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549973 #edb/ref 1074 "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=2>0,$ $b=-4,$ and $c=4.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{(-4)}{2\\cdot 2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=1$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{x}^{2}-4x+4 \\\\  & =2(1)^{2}-4(1)+4 \\\\  & =2-4+4 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(1,2),$ and the range of the function is $[2,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/5f/5f98a1b646aeab2a8a0f0a04014abb0a0a88794d88c880fb771d5f2f65afa4e7.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549983 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549983 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106278" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549983 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549983 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)=(x-4)^{2}+19?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549983 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9acd8689-6770-5d1b-9670-f2ff78d7e31a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549983 #edb/ref 1074 "The parabola is written in vertex form, \n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the leading coefficient and $(h,k)$ is the vertex. \n\n- Since $h=4$ and $k=19,$ the vertex is $(4,19).$\n\n- Since $a=1>0,$ the parabola opens upwards.\n\nBecause the parabola opens upwards, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nTherefore, the range is $[19,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/7f/7fbc9dc7839e439967d6f9f50e25d561ff7be8211b899c83ff269b019b4c61c5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549998 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549998 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90975" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549998 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549998 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549998 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c749b226-6df0-583d-9d39-da82636ab3b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549998 #edb/ref 1074 "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=1>0,$ $b=0,$ and $c=1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{0}{2\\cdot 1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(0,1),$ and the range of the function is $[1,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/0a/0a0bb7cbd5494106b36e85f4b9f2604153d28623883c33f507e1ef36389999c4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550005 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550005 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106274" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550005 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550005 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the domain of the function $h(x)=8{x}^{2}-4x-1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550005 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df827c7e-ce3b-5b89-941a-437f86328b7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550005 #edb/ref 1074 "A quadratic function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the domain of $h(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
